this is a production of cornell  university  so i'll be discussing my research and  detecting genetic and local  environmental effects using  high-throughput  aerial imagery there's a  long history of satellite and airplane  imagery  for applications in agriculture  for example in 1972  when the united states launched um  landsat  1 uh the satellite into orbit  that enabled a lot of remote sensing  applications to to begin and for  instance the  vegetation indices to be developed  um our applications for genetic  improvement  in plant breeding have been hindered by  the cost the resolution of the imagery  and repeatability due to weather or  other positioning problems when you want  to image  the same field throughout the growing  season  so today we have a low low-cost and  stable platform  in the form of unoccupied aerial  vehicles or uavs  and this enables high-resolution images  it's scalable in that a single pilot  or operator can fly um many fields  in one day and and could potentially fly  every day of  the growing season and for instance i  flew  uh 18 flights this summer  of a field in aurora and 28 flights of  alfalfa here in ithaca and and many  others  and i use um or we use the the  microsense multi-spectral camera which  captures in the blue green red red edge  and near infrared  spectra so you can see the camera here  with the five  distinct lenses um  and the thing to remember is to you have  to take a  picture of this calibration panel prior  to flying  and post processing i i do two i follow  two steps  um the first is to generate on ortho  photo mosaic  using fix 4d um i want to  explore using open drone map because  it's a open source  software um then the second step  is to upload those orthophotomosaics to  imagery  for this website i have imagery.org  um so this is a doctor deployable  uh software so you can set up an  instance on your own  um computer if you wanted it's  open source and it houses  all the field genotypic data  and analyses  so in 2019 we have this genomes to  fields  field this was 232  hybrid planted over 500 plots  and with nick kasmor in san antonio we  got  six lights um if you look closely you  can see the  red and blue lines for the plot  delineations  um zooming in you can see the plot these  plot polygons  and um you can see it's fairly high  resolution  and each of these plot polygons is saved  in  the database um i went ahead and  calculated vegetation indices for each  plot  for each time point so i have ndvi  ndre tgi and vari  these vegetation indices are nice  because they're  they're derived simply from means of  images but in the future i want to  find phenotypes that can better capture  features that are late in the image  so that's for a future project  but once i have these phenotypes i  use linear mixed models to  separate genetic from environmental  effects so  for the fixed effect i account for the  replicate  um in the field for the genetic effect  i use um genomic relationships that are  calculated from  close to a million gbs markers that were  genotyped for the parents of these  hybrids  and the random environmental effects can  either  be or spatial effects  or permanent environment effects  so going a little bit deeper into the  the models that i'm looking at  i'm looking at three different classes  of linear mix  models the first class is  is the spatial effects so it can be a  two-dimensional spline  or an auto regressive process and this  class of  linear mix model um  takes information from the rows and the  columns of the plots in the field  and um so then the second class is these  temporal effects or permanent  environment effects and they're  computed using a random regression  and the third class is a random  regression  but i can incorporate um  either spatial effects or  soil survey information or or any  plot to plot correlation structure um  [Music]  could be incorporated in that two-step  random or into that random regression  the outputs of these um models  uh are what you see on the right so  i'll have genetic effects for  each line across time so  each line represents the hybrid and  x-axis is time and  why it accesses the genetic effect and  also  as you see on the bottom right um  i'll have  [Music]  the environmental effect across time so  these are heat  like snapshots of the field of the plots  in the field  over time  and the the random regression has benef  many benefits  um including that it fits a continuous  covariance function  across all the time points so you can  essentially evaluate the genetic or  environmental effect at any given time  point  and it can fit um large numbers of time  points which  multi-trait models um could have  convergence issues with  so with these models um it's there's  still a question of  which one is capturing the effects  um truly and since we don't know  either the genetic or a true genetic or  environmental effect  i i can use simulation um so this is a  three-step  approach where i first take one of those  models  and i minimize the local environment  effect  um or for using real data  then the second step is to add in a  simulated  known effect and the third is to  observe which of these models can most  accurately detect  the the simulated effect  so this is a work in progress and  i should soon have results for  and lastly i want to look if  soil survey uh information can  explain some of the variation this this  local environmental variation  and in 2019 we we have a  electrical conductance measurements and  altitude measurements  that were measured by  nick casmar and so on the left here you  see electrical conductance  and on the right altitude and we can  also compute  like first and second derivatives of of  that of those measurements  so try to account for curvature of the  of the soil or curvature of the field  which is known to be a a source of  variation so in closing  um uavs allow for low-cost  high-resolution phenotypes  image breed can be used to quickly  extract  plot-level phenotypes linear mixed  models can separate  genetic from spatial and temporal or  permanent environment effects  in the field i can use simulation to  probe which model  best captures the true effect  and um soil survey info may  provide um insight into the source of  the effect  so i want to thank my committee um kelly  ellie robbins lucas mueller harold  vanessa and jogens  and um here i'm just  um these are the flights i took in in  this year of the alfalfa so  i'm looking forward to analyzing that  also  so thank you awesome thank you nick for  that great talk  um if anybody has any questions  the chat is open  uh hey nick this is jean-luc um this is  probably an impossible question to  answer but  can you explain briefly the difference  between uh  this the spline model and an auto  aggressive model i'm kind of familiar  with auto aggressive  not too familiar with splines yeah  so the autoregressive assumes a fixed  correlation between uh all of the plots  in the field so  um if you move away one unit of distance  from a plot  you'll follows a correlation  of you know it has a fixed correlation  and if you move away two units it's that  correlation squared  three units a correlation q  um but with the spline it fits a  function um it solves for coefficients  for  linear splines so like it's a  linear equation of the rows and columns  and  their interactions so  i don't know if that answers but  uh thanks yeah that's helpful  okay well thank you and yeah feel free  to  email me or ask me a question  awesome thank you again nick  this has been a production of cornell  university  on the web at cornell.edu

